+# Agent Note: Authenticated control admission
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-10-04-authenticated-control-admission.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+A Lead Agent identity and public Gateway invocation cannot prove HUMAN control intent. Protected approval actions need evidence from actual authenticated operator ingress, with exact action, resolved Agent identity, revision-bound arguments and request lifetime.
+
+## Decision
+
+Connection creates a private request lifetime only after Host/Origin and signed browser-cookie verification succeeds. Gateway binds that lifetime to the exact endpoint, wire argument digest and resolved positional arguments. Protected services consume one-use receipts matching the exact action, Agent object and captured request digest. Receipts become inactive when the authenticated handler settles, so retained asynchronous descendants cannot reuse them. Direct handlers, ordinary programmatic Gateway invocation and nested calls create no lifetime. The signed operator session attests control intent on the protected action; Lead/model identity and unrelated authenticated actions never authorize it.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+A model-visible approval flag or Lead identity supplies no authenticated provenance. Treating RPC interception as proof would trust direct and shell-owned carrier calls that have not passed browser authentication.
+
+## Consequences
+
+GAT owns canonical mission/task authorization and opaque live Agent leases without making the carrier import GAT. Missing, cancelled, mismatched, stale or replayed control admission fails before protected effects. Shell-owned transports need their own authenticated authority adapter and receive no browser provenance merely by calling the shared handler. Trusted typed host package code remains trusted; model/tool JSON cannot mint receipts.
